What PalaceProject does:

```java
USER
  ↓
PALACE MOBILE APP        → UI, Search, Bookshelf, Download Manager, Session
  ↓ (1) GET /opds        → gets the catalog (just metadata, no loan yet)
  ↓ (2) POST /borrow     → actually requests the book

PUBLISHER BACKEND        → Books, Users, Loans, Concurrency, Subscription Logic
  ↓ (3) POST /generate-license

READIUM LCP SERVER       → Encrypts the EPUB, creates the .lcp license file
  ↓ (4) Downloads both

READIUM READER (SDK)     → Validates .lcp, decrypts EPUB, renders, offline reading
  ↓ GET /status

LICENSE STATUS (LSD)     → Real-time: Active / Returned / Revoked / Expired
```

| Step | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | Publisher exposes an OPDS catalog — metadata only (title, author, cover, borrow URL). **No loan, no license yet.** |
| 2 | Palace app fetches `GET /opds` and displays the books |
| 3 | User taps Borrow → app calls `POST /borrow` with userId + bookId |
| 4 | Publisher backend validates: checks concurrency, subscription, checkout limit, user permissions |
| 5 | Publisher calls `POST /generate-license` on the LCP Server → LCP encrypts the EPUB and creates an `.lcp` license file |
| 6 | Palace downloads **two separate files**: `AI.epub` + `AI.lcp` — both stored locally on device |
| 7 | Readium Reader opens the book: validates signature → decrypts EPUB → renders HTML |
| 8 | Once downloaded, EPUB + license are on device — no internet needed to read |
| 9 | License expiry check: `GET /status` → LSD Server replies ACTIVE / RETURNED / REVOKED / EXPIRED |

#### OPDS + Content

Content Type : EPUB + Books

1. displaying all OPDS, later compatible w analytics part to flag unsubscribed books to generate revenue *(mention the latter for business perspective)*
2. OPDS catalog is not generated live on every request. Instead:
    - A **scheduled job** (Lambda or a cron) periodically calls T&F's internal catalog API
    - It builds the OPDS feed JSON
    - It stores that static JSON file in an **S3 bucket**
    - The mobile app fetches from S3 — fast, cheap, no database hit per request
3. filter in catalogue to filter by publisher, access, preview only
4. **Open Access as a differentiator via OPDS:**
    
    OA content has no license requirement. In your OPDS feed, OA items should have no borrow link — just a direct download/read link. The fact that OPDS can clearly surface OA content without any auth friction is presented as a genuine differentiator.
    
5. **cookies** and **pre-signed URLs** — some content may be served via time-limited URLs rather than streaming through your API, which is a common and smart pattern for large files like EPUBs
6. ISBN, DOI, and getFTR — Content Identity
    - **DOI** (Digital Object Identifier) answers: which publisher does this content belong to? Every T&F book and journal article has a DOI. It's the canonical, globally unique ID.
    - **ISBN** is the book-specific identifier — used alongside DOI for book licenses.
    - **getFTR** — this is "Get Full Text Research," a service that helps discovery tools (like university library portals) find the right platform to serve an article. When a user clicks an article link in their library catalog, getFTR routes them to T&F (or whoever has the full text). For your app, getFTR is relevant because it's the existing mechanism that resolves a DOI to a content URL — you might be able to hook into this rather than building your own DOI resolver.
7. Content types (PDF, EPUB, video) will be handled by a common interface, with separate implementations per type. Adding a new content type in future will not require changes to existing screens or download logic.
8. OPDS License Fields

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `checkouts` | Lifetime borrow limit (e.g., max 5 borrows ever) |
| `concurrency` | Max simultaneous users (the "seats" number) |
| `length` | Loan duration (e.g., 21 days) |
| `expires` | Exact expiry date/time |
| `format` | EPUB / PDF / LCP / etc. |
| `devices` | Device limit per user |
| `copy` | Can user copy text? |
| `print` | Can user print? |
| `tts` | Text-to-speech allowed? |

#### License and Entitlement

1. Valid Flow: User opens book → App calls Content API → Content API calls License Service AND Entitlement Service → both pass → Content API returns the file → App renders content”

```java
Mobile App
    │
    ▼
API Gateway ──── Auth Service ──── Users DB
    │
    ├──→ Content API ──────────────────────── S3 (EPUBs + OPDS feed)
    │         │
    │         ├──→ License Service ─────────── Licenses DB (MongoDB)
    │         │
    │         └──→ Entitlement Service ──────── Bundles DB (MongoDB/Neo4j)
    │
    └──→ Notification Service ─────────────── (license expiry alerts)
```

```java
User opens book
       │
       ▼
  Content API          ← the orchestrator, calls both
    │        │
    ▼        ▼
License    Entitlement
Service    Service
    │        │
    ▼        ▼
Is today   Is this book
between    in their
start/end? package?
    │        │
    └───┬────┘
        ▼
   Both pass?
   → Return content
     (S3 URL or file)
```

1. the license API is called at **open time**, not at download time. The file may be on your device already — but every time you open it, it checks the license. This is how expiry enforcement works offline — the license itself contains the end date, so the app can check it without a network call.
